# Every layer whose holder shares are all stated, beside the magnitude they must add up to. One
# row per examined layer, `violates` where the sum misses.
#
# The shares sum to the magnitude
#   The rule: the stated shares sum to the magnitude of the remainder. H is a distribution, so
#   its row must account for the whole of what there was to bear: no more, no less.
#
# One unstated share suspends the check
#   It does not break it, and the difference is the whole discipline. If a filer says one party's
#   share is `unmeasured`, the sum is unknown, not wrong. Treating the absence as a zero would
#   manufacture a shortfall and report the document for a defect the checker introduced.
#
# The unit guard is half of this rule
#   Its own site carries the other half. arithmetic/shares_sum.sqlc reports `not comparable`
#   where a holder's share is quoted in a unit other than its layer's; without that branch here
#   the rule sums across units and compares the total to |r|. H is a distribution over the
#   magnitude of `r`, so a share in a foreign unit is not a smaller share, it is not a share of
#   this at all. arithmetic/roster.sqlc records `guarded_by = NULL` for that site: no unit rule
#   stands there, which leaves this check as the only thing between it and a wrong number. It
#   agrees with the site on this corpus only because no such document exists yet.
#
# The whole interval, not the middle number
#   Comparing `abs(r_mode)` against `shares_mode` and nothing else ignores that a quantity is a
#   range, which is this model's entire thesis. Three other rules still do arithmetic on the mode
#   alone.
#
# Containment at the ends, equality at the mode
#   The asymmetry is not a compromise: requiring equality at the ends would accuse a more careful
#   filing. The magnitude is read from layers/remainder.sqlc, which is the remainder in force, so on
#   a composed layer it is the pivoted `F Φ r - e_n + e_d` and not `(Σn) − (Σd)`.
#   `merge-holding-composition/compute`'s shares [1414, 2857, 4085.6] therefore equal its magnitude
#   at all three points. The crossed difference of its composed totals, [1092, 2857, 4198.8], treats
#   the two sums as independent and is looser at both ends; a rule reading it would accept shares
#   the remainder does not allow. Proof: `src/proofs/README.md`, entries `share_sum` and
#   `magnitude`.
#
# |r| over an interval needs the straddle case
#   Where `r` crosses zero the magnitude reaches 0, which is the transition fit stated as
#   arithmetic: short at the top of the demand range and spare at the bottom, both at once.
#   Taking `least(|r_low|, |r_high|)` there would invent a floor the layer does not have.
#
# Probe
#   Widen `enterprise-contract/support-cover`'s only share from [21, 50, 76] to [21, 50, 300],
#   on a layer whose whole nameplate is 168, leaving the mode where it is. This rule must fire.
-- pm:Remainder/pm:holder summed against |r| from layers/remainder.sqlc, via entries/holder_totals.sqlc.
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT x.filing, x.layer,
           abs(x.shares - x.magnitude) > 1e-9
           OR x.shares_low  < x.mag_low  - 1e-9
           OR x.shares_high > x.mag_high + 1e-9                       AS violates,
           format('shares [%s, %s, %s] against a magnitude of [%s, %s, %s]',
                  x.shares_low, x.shares, x.shares_high,
                  x.mag_low, x.magnitude, x.mag_high)                 AS detail
    FROM (
        SELECT r.filing, r.layer,
               r.m_mode       AS magnitude,
               h.shares_mode  AS shares,
               h.shares_low, h.shares_high,
               r.m_low        AS mag_low,
               r.m_high       AS mag_high
        FROM      (
            :compose(layers/remainder.sqlc)
        ) r
        JOIN      (
            :compose(entries/holder_totals.sqlc)
        ) h USING (filing, layer)
        WHERE h.unstated = 0
          AND h.share_units = ARRAY[r.unit]
    ) x
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'shares_do_not_sum'
